## Why This Is Asked

Real-time, asynchronous, event-driven integrations with external partners are inherently error-prone. This question evaluates understanding of contract testing, schema evolution strategy, latency SLAs, idempotency, and resilience patterns—all critical for payment infrastructure reliability.

## Key Concepts

- Consumer-driven contracts (Pact) for multi-party API agreements
- Backward-compatible schema evolution with feature-flagged payloads
- Async webhook reliability: retries, dead-letter queues, circuit breakers
- End-to-end latency measurement with OpenTelemetry distributed tracing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pact contract for webhook payload versioning
const { Pact } = require('@pact-foundation/pact');

const provider = new Pact({
  consumer: 'fraud-gateway',
  provider: 'partner-webhook',
  cors: true,
});

describe('Partner Webhook Contract', () => {
  before(() => provider.setup());
  after(() => provider.finalize());

  it('accepts v2 schema with optional risk fields', async () => {
    await provider.addInteraction({
      state: 'partner supports v2 schema',
      uponReceiving: 'a fraud score webhook',
      withRequest: {
        method: 'POST',
        path: '/partners/123/webhook',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: {
          transactionId: 'txn_abc123',
          riskScore: 85,
          newField: 'feature-flag-value', // v2-only field
        },
      },
      willRespondWith: {
        status: 200,
        body: { received: true },
      },
    });
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Streaming APIs combined with REST surfaces are common in large-scale systems. This question probes testing of delivery guarantees, ordering, resilience under partial outages, and observability—areas critical for Snap, Microsoft, and Google-scale services.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing across REST and WebSocket channels
- Message ordering and sequencing guarantees
- Delivery semantics (at-least-once vs exactly-once) and replay safety
- Reconnection, backoff, and backfill strategies using durable offsets
- Handling partial upstream outages and dynamic schema changes
- Observability with tracing, logs, and metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: basic WebSocket stream ordering test (conceptual)
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/stream');
let lastSeq = -1;
let ok = true;
let count = 0;
ws.on('message', (data) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(data);
  if (typeof msg.seq !== 'number' || msg.seq <= lastSeq) ok = false;
  lastSeq = msg.seq;
  count++;
  if (count >= 100) ws.close();
});
```

